---
/**
 * 判断事例 — what the method looks like when it is applied to one problem.
 *
 * #31. HOW I BUILD states a claim — 「AI へ実装を委譲しても、理解まで委譲しない。」
 * — and until now the page supported it with a workflow diagram and a roles
 * table. Both describe an intention. Neither lets a reader check one.
 *
 * So this section carries no new claim. It transcribes two public pull
 * requests in this repository and sends the reader to them: what was there,
 * what investigation established, what was decided, what it became, and how it
 * was verified. Every sentence is in `site.howIBuild.decisionCases` with the
 * PR sections it came from; nothing is written here.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SECTION DOES NOT SAY. It never labels an initial design as an AI
 * proposal. PR #18 and PR #20 record what was measured, rejected, chosen and
 * verified; they do not record who first suggested the design each one starts
 * from. Writing 「AI案」 over those would be the site adding a claim to its own
 * sources — the exact move the section exists to argue against. The first
 * field says what the situation WAS, with no origin attached to it.
 *
 * TWO LAYERS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM FOLDS (HD-L). 問題 / 確認した事実 / 判断 /
 * 結果 are always open: they are the case, and a conclusion behind a disclosure
 * triangle is a conclusion most readers never see. 検証 — the measurements,
 * the test counts, the conditions — is a `<details>`, because a reader who
 * wants it is a reader who has already decided to check, and they can. The PR
 * link sits OUTSIDE the fold for the same reason: the way to verify this page
 * must not itself be hidden.
 *
 * #52 — THE QA RECORD IS NOT IN THIS FILE ANY MORE.
 *
 * It used to be the last block inside this section: a third `.dc` under the
 * same head, after two cases, which is exactly the reading HD-J ruled out. The
 * component said so in a comment and the DOM said the opposite. It is now its
 * own section in `HowIBuildQaRecord.astro`, and the boundary is a file rather
 * than a comment — this component renders `site.howIBuild.decisionCases` and
 * has no way to render anything else, so the count it draws is the count the
 * data states.
 */
import { publicPrUrl } from '../../lib/content/derive.ts';
import { site } from '../../lib/content/site.ts';
import { fill, ui } from '../../lib/content/ui.ts';

const cases = site.howIBuild.decisionCases;

/**
 * The id the section's `aria-labelledby` points at, written once.
 *
 * Two places have to agree on it and they are 30 lines apart; a literal typed
 * twice is a region that silently loses its name the day one of them changes.
 */
const HEADING_ID = 'decision-cases-head';

/**
 * The four always-open fields, in reading order, paired with their label.
 *
 * A list rather than four hand-written rows: the order is the argument (what
 * happened → what was established → what was chosen → what it became) and
 * writing it once means the two cases cannot drift into stating it differently.
 */
const fields = [
  { label: ui.howIBuild.caseProblem, of: (c: (typeof cases)[number]) => c.problem },
  { label: ui.howIBuild.caseObserved, of: (c: (typeof cases)[number]) => c.observed },
  { label: ui.howIBuild.caseDecision, of: (c: (typeof cases)[number]) => c.decision },
  { label: ui.howIBuild.caseResult, of: (c: (typeof cases)[number]) => c.result },
];
---

{/*
  #31 — THE SECTION IS NAMED BY ITS HEADING, ONCE.

  It carried `aria-label="判断事例"` and printed the same word again as a rail
  label, which named the region twice for a screen reader and gave the document
  outline no heading at all: the two decision cases were `h3`s hanging under
  開発の前提, a section they are not part of. Now the word appears exactly once,
  as the `<h2>` the outline needs, and `aria-labelledby` points the region at
  it instead of repeating it.

  It is drawn as the section head the rest of this page already uses — a mono
  label on the 2px rule, the same instrument as ROLES above it. `.mo` outranks
  `.ad h2` on every property that would have made it a 40px display line, so
  the heading is a heading in the markup and unchanged on the page.
*/}
<section class="s-open" id="decision-cases" aria-labelledby={HEADING_ID}>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="tr">
      <div class="ins">
        <span class="lb">{fill(ui.howIBuild.casesCount, { count: cases.length })}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fld">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2 class="mo" id={HEADING_ID}><b>{ui.howIBuild.casesLabel}</b></h2>
        </div>
        {/*
          #52 — ONE SENTENCE BEFORE THE FIRST CASE.

          The head was followed immediately by a case title, so the first thing
          a reader was given was the conclusion of a specific incident, and the
          question the section answers — what IS a 判断事例 here — was only
          answerable by finishing both of them. This sentence answers it first.

          It is not a claim and it is not a summary of the cases. It names the
          five fields below in the order they appear, which is the only thing
          this section promises. The wording is the owner's, approved in
          Issue #52 comment 5791022793, and it is read from the registry like
          every other shipping string.
        */}
        <p class="dc-lead">{ui.howIBuild.casesLead}</p>
        {
          cases.map((c) => (
            <article class="dc" data-decision-case={c.id} data-pr={c.prNumber}>
              <h3 class="dc-t">{c.title}</h3>
              {/* A description list, because that is what these are: a term
                  and what it holds. The label is the mono instrument face the
                  rest of the site uses for a field name, so a reader who has
                  read one block on this site can read this one. */}
              <dl class="dc-s">
                {fields.map((f) => (
                  <>
                    <dt class="mo"><b>{f.label}</b></dt>
                    <dd>{f.of(c)}</dd>
                  </>
                ))}
              </dl>
              <details class="dc-v">
                <summary>{ui.howIBuild.caseVerification}</summary>
                <ul>{c.verification.map((v) => <li>{v}</li>)}</ul>
              </details>
              <p class="dc-l m0">
                <a class="lk" href={publicPrUrl(c.prNumber)} data-decision-pr={c.prNumber}>
                  {ui.howIBuild.openPr}<span class="ar">→</span>
                </a>
              </p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
